<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android has blocked cleartext HTTP by default since API 28, and every LAN
  server Flower can find is cleartext: an mDNS sighting is turned into
  "http://<ip>:<port>" (NetworkDiscoveryService.HttpOrigin) because the peer
  is on this link, has no name a certificate could be issued for, and pairing
  happens over the same link anyway. Without this file the Android app
  discovers servers in the sidebar and then fails every request to them - the
  device checks found exactly that, with every local-file decode green and
  every streamed one reporting "the track would not open".

  What this file cannot do is say "only private ranges". A network security
  config matches hostnames and DNS suffixes; there is no CIDR form, and the
  addresses that need permitting are IP literals handed over by mDNS, so
  there is nothing to enumerate. Permitting cleartext at the base config is
  therefore Android's only answer here, and the private-range rule lives
  where it can actually be written down instead: CleartextOrigins, which
  refuses an http origin whose host is not loopback, link-local, RFC1918 or a
  tailnet address. That check runs on every platform rather than only this
  one, which is the better place for it regardless of what the manifest could
  express.

  Trust anchors are left at the platform default on purpose. Flower's extra
  rule - a server certificate is acceptable if its public key is one this
  device has paired with - is PeerHttpClient's, applied in managed code per
  connection, and putting a second, coarser trust decision here would only
  widen what the pin narrows.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
